__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
dist/
build/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
tests/fixtures/*.jsonl
*.out.jsonl
telemetry.jsonl
.DS_Store

# Agent worktrees. Tooling that runs work in parallel checks each task out into
# its own worktree under here, and a worktree is a nested git repository: `git
# add -A` stages it as a gitlink, which clones cannot resolve and which quietly
# records one machine's scratch directory as repository content. Seen in
# practice -- a bulk `git add -A` during a trial merge staged seven of them.
# Nothing under .claude/ is a build input or an artefact anyone should receive.
.claude/

# lab build artefacts: user-data contains the password hash and SSH key
lab/generated/
lab/*.local.psd1

# The local lab's ledger lock. Everything else under lab/local/runs/ IS
# committed on purpose -- it is the evidence the run produced and CI re-derives
# it -- but a POSIX advisory lock file is a fact about one process, not a result.
lab/local/runs/**/*.lock

# The generated evaluation corpus: about 41 MB, deterministic from the seed in
# eval/corpus/generate.py, so committing it would store nothing the generator
# does not already encode. Regenerate with `python eval/corpus/generate.py`.
# A readable sample (one session per family x kind) IS committed, at
# eval/corpus/sample.jsonl, so the corpus can be audited without running it.
eval/corpus/data/

# The Ed25519 signature cache. Derived, content-addressed, and safe to delete at
# any time -- see eval/corpus/signatures.py for why it has no stale state to go
# wrong. Outside data/ on purpose: that directory is digested into the
# evaluation card, and a cache is not a corpus artefact.
eval/corpus/.signature-cache.json
